1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,708
Budgeting as a couple?

2
00:00:02,127 --> 00:00:08,245
There are three main systems. Let's run real numbers through all three, and see what fair actually looks like.

3
00:00:08,665 --> 00:00:14,341
Our example couple: Alex takes home $4,000 a month. Sam takes home $2,500.

4
00:00:14,761 --> 00:00:17,231
Their shared bills come to $3,250 a month.

5
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:19,679
Three systems. Same couple.

6
00:00:20,449 --> 00:00:23,012
System one. Split everything fifty-fifty.

7
00:00:23,432 --> 00:00:29,742
Each pays $1,625. Alex has $2,375 left. Sam has $875.

8
00:00:30,162 --> 00:00:36,047
It sounds fair. But it takes 65% of Sam's pay, and only 41% of Alex's.

9
00:00:36,817 --> 00:00:38,773
System two. Split by income.

10
00:00:39,193 --> 00:00:50,449
Alex earns about 62% of the total, so Alex pays about 62% of the bills: $2,000. Sam pays $1,250. Now both pay exactly half their income.

11
00:00:51,219 --> 00:00:53,201
System three. One shared pot.

12
00:00:53,621 --> 00:01:03,222
Both paychecks go in: $6,500. Minus the bills, $3,250. Minus $300 of fun money for Alex. Minus $300 for Sam.

13
00:01:03,642 --> 00:01:06,341
$2,650 a month goes to shared goals.

14
00:01:07,111 --> 00:01:08,234
So which one fits?

15
00:01:08,654 --> 00:01:22,923
Fifty-fifty works when incomes are similar, or the relationship is newer. Split by income when pay is different. One pot fits couples with fully shared goals. And in every system, each person gets some money with no questions asked.

16
00:01:23,343 --> 00:01:30,221
Fair doesn't have to mean equal dollars. It means a deal you both agree is fair. And talk numbers once a month, together.

17
00:01:30,991 --> 00:01:34,835
The Paycheck Planner in the description works for one paycheck, or two.

18
00:01:35,255 --> 00:01:39,665
Subscribe. Next time: one month in, and how to fix the budget you started.
